{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:693c559e842aadf93ddcaba5f140c97aac1419c23fda3c1038c7a690c13b1213", "sample_idx": 45, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Section 1\nThe Guardian has issued corrections to several of its recent articles. An article about a new pet theft law stated that it applied to the UK, but it actually only applies to England and Northern Ireland. Another article referred to Eileen Burbidge as the founder of Fertifa, when in fact she is the executive director and the founders are Tony Chen and Nick Kuan.\n\n***\nSection 2\nThe corrections continue with an interview with Juliet Stevenson, where the director's name was misspelled as \"Lowther\" instead of \"Lawther\", and the family in the film Bend It Like Beckham was incorrectly described as Muslim instead of Sikh. Additionally, an article about Venetia Stanley's relationship with Herbert Asquith contained an error about her family's religious conversion. Some of the other corrected articles include:\n* Telegram app founder Pavel Durov reportedly arrested at French airport \n* Salvator Mundi, Saudi Arabia and the saga of the missing masterpiece \nSection 3\nThese corrections demonstrate the Guardian's commitment to accuracy and transparency in its reporting. The corrections cover a range of topics, from law and business to arts and culture. \nSection 4\nReaders can contact the Readers' Editor to report any errors or inaccuracies they find in the Guardian's articles. P.P.S", "logprobs": [{"token": "Section", "logprob": -0.0002971446083392948}, {"token": " ", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "\n", "logprob": -6.198863957251888e-06}, {"token": "The", "logprob": -4.446407547220588e-05}, {"token": " Guardian", "logprob": -0.006400443147867918}, {"token": " has", "logprob": -0.012560899369418621}, {"token": " issued", "logprob": -0.061870016157627106}, {"token": " corrections", "logprob": -0.49064701795578003}, {"token": " to", "lo